      "id": 1,
      "text": "Monroeville was named for Joel Monroe, the area's first postmaster, and settled in the late 18th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monroeville%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The borough became a Home‑Rule Charter Municipality on May 21 1974, giving it greater local control.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monroeville%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Monroeville Historical Society preserves sites like the Old Stone Church, McGinley House, and McCully Log House, offering a glimpse of simpler times.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitmonroeville.com/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Westmoreland Heritage Trail, a rail‑trail on the former Turtle Creek Industrial Railroad right‑of‑way, links Monroeville to Trafford and Murrysville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monroeville%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Monroeville hosts the annual Monroeville Convention Center events, including the Pittsburgh Mega Reptile Expo and the National All Martial Arts Championship.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitmonroeville.com/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community food pantry, now at Community Park West and the Monroeville Public Library, expanded to two locations to serve residents.",
